\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Stage 3.4/3.5 are now shared-edge topological assembly
\f0 \'a0\'97 the \'a73.x design, with sewing gone entirely. The core mechanism is uniform across all five surface types:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 One\'a0
\f3\fs26 \cb5 TopoDS_Edge
\f2\fs28 \cb3 \'a0per ReconEdge, shared by both faces.
\f0 \'a0Wires, faces, and shells are built with\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 BRep_Builder
\f0\fs28 \cb3 ;\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 MakeWire
\f0\fs28 \cb3 /
\f1\fs26 \cb5 MakeFace
\f0\fs28 \cb3 -from-wire/
\f1\fs26 \cb5 ShapeFix
\f0\fs28 \cb3 \'a0(which copy edges \'97 the root of the old fragility) are gone from face construction. Shells are closed by construction.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Analytic charts
\f0 \'a0(\cf6 \strokec6 SurfaceChart\cf2 \strokec2 ) replace\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 ProjectPointOnSurf
\f0\fs28 \cb3 \'a0for all UV work. Boundary loops are branch-aligned in UV and their\'a0
\f2 winding
\f0 \'a0(\'b11 around a periodic direction) decides which faces need seams \'97 no span heuristics.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Seams are anchored at shared vertices
\f0 \'a0(existing vertex, or an anchor added to the shared closed edge), so adjacent wrapped faces agree on the seam azimuth\'a0
\f4\i by construction
\f0\i0 . Seam 3D curves come from\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 u_iso
\f0\fs28 \cb3 /
\f1\fs26 \cb5 v_iso
\f0\fs28 \cb3 , which works for any surface type including future NURBS.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Degenerate pole edges
\f0 \'a0are the single pole mechanism for cone apexes and sphere poles. Sphere axes now point at the region's mean direction (a pure gauge choice), which eliminated every pole-proximity special case.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Pcurves
\f0 \'a0interpolate analytic samples of each edge's stored curve, snap endpoints to shared-vertex UVs, and set edge tolerances from measured deviation\'a0
\f4\i including endpoints
\f0\i0 \'a0\'97 which is exactly what OCCT's in-context BRepCheck validates.\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 ReconEdge.pcurves
\f0\fs28 \cb3 \'a0and\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 BRepVertex.uv_coords
\f0\fs28 \cb3 \'a0are finally populated as \'a73.0 intended.\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stage 3.3 bonus:
\f0 \'a0edge curves are now validated against the mesh boundary chain and repaired with the chain's circle when they stray. This caught IntSS returning curves 14 mm off the junction between the near-tangent coaxial cones.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

