\cf2 \cb7 Describe the issue with edge splitting for incompatible seam anchors\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Located the incompatible-anchor error for reference\cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 It's the one known unsatisfiable case in the seam-planning scheme, guarded by a hard error at\'a0\cf6 \strokec6 stage3.rs:4642\cf2 \strokec2 . Here's the full picture:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs30 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 The invariant that creates the problem\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 A face whose boundary wraps a periodic direction (a full-revolution cylinder band, a torus fillet ring, an apex cap) needs a\'a0
\f2 seam edge
\f0 \'a0\'97 an isoline at some azimuth \'97 to close its UV boundary. For the wire to be topologically valid, the seam's endpoints must be\'a0
\f2 vertices of the winding boundary loops
\f0 ; a vertex can't sit in the interior of another edge. So every winding loop of a wrapped face needs a vertex exactly where the seam crosses it. That vertex is the loop's\'a0
\f4\i anchor
\f0\i0 .\cb1 \
\cb3 Since a face has exactly one seam, and the seam is an isoline at a single azimuth, a face with two winding loops (band between two circles) needs\'a0
\f2 both loops' anchors at the same wrap coordinate
\f0 \'a0in its chart.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs30 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Why anchors can conflict\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Anchors come in two kinds with very different freedom:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A\'a0
\f2 vertex-free closed edge
\f0 \'a0(a plain junction circle) is a gauge: we rebuild it to start anywhere and put the anchor vertex there. Free choice \'97 but only once, because the anchor becomes part of the\'a0
\f4\i shared
\f0\i0 \'a0edge, and both adjacent faces see it.\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A\'a0
\f2 real BRepVertex
\f0 \'a0already on the loop (a corner where 3+ surfaces meet) is a geometric fact. It cannot move.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Because anchors live on shared edges, the choice propagates: planning face A's seam pins the anchor on circle E; face B, which also wraps and shares E, must put\'a0
\f4\i its
\f0\i0 \'a0seam through that same point, which pins the anchor on B's other loop, and so on down the chain.\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 plan_seam_anchors
\f0\fs28 \cb3 \'a0does this in waves \'97 faces with an already-determined anchor plan first \'97 and for coaxial stacks (plane\'96cylinder\'96torus\'96\'85, which is every model in the zoo) the constraints are always mutually satisfiable, because "same azimuth" means the same thing on every face in the chain.\cb1 \
\cb3 The failure is when a face's two winding loops\'a0
\f2 both arrive pre-determined
\f0 \'a0\'97 real vertices, or shared edges already anchored by earlier-planned neighbors \'97 at azimuths that differ by more than the 0.01 rad congruence tolerance. No single isoline passes through both anchors, so no valid seam placement exists, and the code errors with "winding loops have incompatible seam anchors \'85 edge splitting not implemented" rather than producing broken topology.\cb1 \
\cb3 Two concrete geometries would trigger it:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Vertex-split boundary circles at misaligned azimuths.
\f0 \'a0A cylinder band whose top circle is split by real vertices at 0\'b0 and 180\'b0 (some feature touches there) while the bottom circle is split at 90\'b0 and 270\'b0. All anchors immovable, none aligned.\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\f2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Cycles of wrapped faces with holonomy.
\f0 \'a0A closed circuit of surfaces of revolution around\'a0
\f4\i non-coaxial
\f0\i0 \'a0axes \'97 think a closed picture-frame pipe of straight cylinders and elbow tori. Each face rigidly maps "azimuth on one junction circle" to "azimuth on the next"; around a cycle the composition can return rotated, so no globally consistent assignment exists. (There's also a subtler third path: a closed-but-non-periodic curve, e.g. a closed IntSS BSpline, can't be re-ranged to a new start \'97 the code keeps its existing start with a warning, and if that start later conflicts, same error.)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs30 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 What edge splitting would do\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 The recorded fix: when a face needs its seam at azimuth \uc0\u952  and a winding loop has no movable vertex there,\'a0
\f2 split the shared edge at the point with wrap-coordinate \uc0\u952 
\f0 \'a0\'97 insert a new vertex, turning one edge into two arcs. Because the split happens on the shared\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 TopoDS_Edge
\f0\fs28 \cb3 /
\f1\fs26 \cb5 ReconEdge
\f0\fs28 \cb3 \'a0before any face is assembled, both adjacent faces see the same two sub-edges: sharing is preserved, the neighbor's wire just contains two arcs instead of one, and pcurves are computed per sub-edge like any other. A loop could then carry anchors at as many azimuths as its neighbors demand.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs30 \cf4 \cb3 \strokec4 Why I deferred it\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Three reasons, in decreasing importance: no current model reaches the case, so the implementation would be dead code with zero test coverage \'97 exactly the kind of speculative machinery the rework removed; the NURBS pass won't reach it either, since\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 PointsToBSplineSurface
\f0\fs28 \cb3 \'a0patches are non-periodic, so NURBS faces never wrap and never need seams; and it's the one extension that mutates the ReconEdge-level topology mid-assembly (edge lists, loop walks, and vertex adjacency all need updating), so it deserves a motivating model to develop against. The hard error message names the mechanism, so whoever first hits it inherits the plan rather than a mystery.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb7 What kind of model or surface would require it?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Three concrete classes of parts would hit it, in roughly increasing order of geometric depth. The common precondition for all of them: a face must\'a0
\f4\i genuinely wrap
\f0\i0 \'a0a periodic direction (needs a seam), and both of its winding boundary loops must arrive with anchors that are already pinned \'97 by real vertices, or by seams of neighboring wrapped faces that share those loops' edges.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 \cb3 1. A pipe crossed by two branch pipes (double-T manifold).
\f0 \'a0When two equal-ish cylinders intersect non-tangentially, the junction is a closed saddle curve \'97 IntSS returns it as a closed but\'a0
\f4\i non-periodic
\f0\i0 \'a0B-spline, which the planner can't re-range to an arbitrary start (evaluating a non-periodic curve outside its stored range is invalid), so its anchor is stuck at the curve's existing start point. A single T-joint survives: the branch face has one stuck loop plus one free end circle, and the free one aligns to the stuck one. But the run-pipe segment\'a0
\f4\i between
\f0\i0 \'a0two branches is a wrapped cylinder whose two winding loops are both stuck saddle curves, each anchored wherever IntSS happened to start its parameterization \'97 generically at different azimuths. This is the most likely first tripper in practice, and notably it's partly an implementation limitation rather than deep geometry: it could also be fixed by rebuilding closed IntSS B-splines as periodic curves (making them re-rangeable), which is cheaper than full edge splitting.\cb1 \

\f2 \cb3 2. A bore with mis-clocked features at its two ends.
\f0 \'a0Real vertices appear on a junction circle wherever a third surface touches it. Picture a through-hole whose top rim is nicked by a slot running north-south and whose bottom rim is nicked by a slot running east-west \'97 with the slots touching only the rims, so the bore's cylindrical face still wraps. The top circle is now a chain of arcs with vertices at 0\'b0/180\'b0, the bottom at 90\'b0/270\'b0. All four anchors are immovable geometric facts, and no single generator line of the cylinder passes through a top vertex and a bottom vertex. Any "clocked" part \'97 cross-drilled holes, keyway notches, lugs \'97 where the angular features at the two ends of a full cylinder or cone don't line up is in this class.\cb1 \

\f2 \cb3 3. A closed pipe circuit routed in 3D (holonomy).
\f0 \'a0Chains of wrapped faces transport the anchor azimuth from one junction circle to the next: along a cylinder, the anchor's azimuth is preserved; around an elbow torus, it's carried by rotation about the elbow axis. In an\'a0
\f4\i open
\f0\i0 \'a0chain, or a\'a0
\f4\i closed planar
\f0\i0 \'a0circuit (a picture-frame duct), these transports compose to the identity, so propagation always finds a consistent assignment \'97 that's why every current model works. But a closed circuit that leaves the plane \'97 a duct run shaped like a skew polygon, three elbows bending in different planes before closing \'97 composes rotations about non-parallel axes, and the net rotation around the loop is generically nonzero. This is genuine parallel-transport holonomy (the same reason a ribbon's frame doesn't close around a non-planar curve). The last face planned in the cycle inherits two anchors that disagree by exactly the holonomy angle, and no seam-anchoring scheme without splitting can fix that: it's a topological obstruction, not a bookkeeping one.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 What\'a0
\f4\i doesn't
\f0\i0 \'a0trigger it \'97 and why the zoo never has: coaxial stacks (every fillet/boss/hole model \'97 all azimuth constraints agree by symmetry), planar closed circuits, single T-joints, and any part where features interrupt the wrap itself (a keyway cut through the full side wall makes the face partial-revolution, so no seam and no anchors are needed at all). And the NURBS pass stays clear of it entirely, since B-spline patches are non-periodic and never wrap.\cb1 \
\cb3 If you ever want to pull the fix forward, a good forcing test model would be class 1 or 2 \'97 both are easy to build in\'a0
\f1\fs26 \cb5 gen-test-models
\f0\fs28 \cb3 \'a0with OCCT booleans, whereas the holonomy circuit is the one that also stresses the propagation order.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
